- Summary:: Oliver Burkeman announces his new book "Meditations for Mortals" available for preorder, focusing on embracing imperfection and making time for what matters in a volatile world. The book offers daily chapters with practical tools for confronting reality and taking action while defying inner standards through an approach called "imperfectionism." Burkeman encourages readers to laugh in the face of standards, redefine success as boldly defying expectations, and embrace authenticity and vitality in their actions.

## Highlights
- What you might find more effective is the imperfectionist power-move I’ve come to think of as **laughing in the face of standards.** 
  Misery-inducing internal standards can be sneaky, because they don’t always resemble stereotypical perfectionism. Certainly, the feeling that you must produce flawless work will make you miserable, because flawlessness is impossible. But so will other kinds of inner demand. Perhaps well-meaning parents told you you could do anything if you set your mind to it – so now you’re convinced that whatever’s lacking from your life must be due to insufficient effort. Maybe you’re genuinely proud of your accomplishments – but then you turn them into a millstone around your neck, because now you feel obligated to keep meeting or exceeding the standard you’d previously set. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j2fzx6qq5k4wmrm5157dk9ab))
- adopt a stance of deliberate defiance toward the inner taskmaster, as a spirited kind of game – one in which you actively cultivate a sense of relish in completely disregarding the standards that usually exert such airless oppression over your days. This is ‘laughing in the face of standards.’ ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j2fzxqz2yv67jrvnx6sstd13))
- The only way through the conundrum was to adopt a mindset of vigorously and flagrantly and thrillingly *defying* the very notion of meeting any standards – writing stuff I sometimes hated, and somehow managing to relish the fact that I hated it. Did I enjoy myself, at least? No! That was another standard I had to throw out of the window. To be honest, for a month or three there, I had a bad time of it. But that was fine. It felt freeing not to *need* to be enjoying it – and paradoxically, that was somewhat enjoyable. I wasn’t going to let the inner taskmaster impose any standards on me at all, and that included the standard of “having fun.” ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j2fzyz2wfd46as564tys5rtw))
- Life flows through you, into real-world action, with fewer obstructions than before. You no longer wait to act until you feel entirely calm and focused, or until you know exactly what you’re doing, or until the world political situation has calmed down. You just *act*, in the wholehearted spirit of ‘here goes nothing!’ Because otherwise what’s the point of being alive? ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j2g00dmxn49v35ygnq3xahzz))
